ghly probable  the bit I played won't even be in the  future game should it even get made so  for all that waiting I got to see an  outline of a thing that might exist and  a commemorative pin after I recounted  that story Daniel Floyd of extra credits  asked me the most natural question  imaginable was it worth it and I don't  know I don't know because I don't  remember the hours of standing in line  have been compressed into single point  memories that no longer hold any weight  it's not a bad time it's just time that  has effectively vanished  from Calgary Southwest to the Crowsnest  past West to ferny and Cranbrook south  to East port bonners ferry pend oreille  and Spokane West to sprog Ritz Ville  Ellensburg and into Seattle from Seattle  north through Linwood Everett Mount  Vernon Abbotsford East to Chilliwack  Bridal Falls and hope north to maraton  Kamloops East to Salmon Arm  SiC a moose Revelstoke Golden Banff and  at long last Sun long set in to Calgary  I've passed through all of this I know  this because the evidence says I clearly  must have but the spaces in between are  gone now in the end the tens of minutes  I spent catching up with friends  chatting about the state of online media  with other video producers and dinner  with some new friends of the Cheesecake  cafe these are the things that I'm more  likely to retain these are the things  that PACs will likely become in my  memories are 45 minutes of good  conversation worth 28 hours of driving  what if you'll barely remember them  content-wise I suppose pax was fine I'm  not sure because I ignored the vast  majority of it there's already so much  standing in line for the things I would  like to see or do or collect that I  really can't be bothered to stand in  line for things I'm at best passively  curious about whether that waiting will  be ejected for memory or not part of  this is that I'm generally more  interested in the overall trends what  are the repeating patterns the running  themes across the convention as a whole  the other part is that the big-name  demos are a bit of a scam the incentives  are all set up to cheat everyone  creators and audience of authenticity  like a dating site where everyone picks  their best photo and lists their flaws  is caring too much and loving too deeply  the convention floor is only friendly to  the shallowest safest easiest to digest  presentation packed into a capsule that  will allow you to process as many bodies  as possible in a limited period of time  spectacle in and of itself isn't  entirely without merit but it suffers  harsh diminishing returns there's only  so many times you can pass the giant  branded dinosaur statue before it stops  being a wonder and becomes a convenient  landmark between the merch booth and the  skybridge virtual reality was  predictably the capital letters big  thing this year it's interesting over  the years watching VR slowly get better  and sit now on the cusp of actually  being good it's still young but slowly  everyone is working out the kinesthetic  dialect of virtual reality along with  the  sanitary logistics of sharing a VR  headset with hundreds of people per day  for the next couple of generations of VR  though the big experience of the matrix  you'll be replicating is having a giant  cable hanging off the back of your head  I can't help but feel a sense of irony  in that following in the footsteps of  the Wiis motion controls and the kinect  and adding almost a decade of  improvements the vive and oculus aim to  free players from the constraints of the  couch allowing them to not just wave  their arms at their television but to  walk around realized immersive fictional  spaces only to then be tethered to  reality by a literal tether of cables  virtual reality is increasingly  impressive but the scenes show quickly  as practical issues rear their head you  put on the headset and you are free to  walk around anywhere anywhere that is  within an 8-foot cube as you approach  the edge you're confronted with a grid  marking the edge of the playable area a  handy guide for preventing you from  breaking things or hurting yourself but  a sudden reminder of the disconnect  between perceived space and the actual  distances in one demo I repeatedly  punched a cloth wall while trying to  pick up a fake paint can off a virtual  shelf sitting against the edge of the  simulated world the reality of virtual  reality is that we're still trying to  figure out the best way of going  somewhere while not going very far it's  the middle space between standard  control dialects and augmented reality  like Pokemon go if you want to go  somewhere in no man's sky you sit in  your chair and push forward on the  controls if you want to go somewhere in  Pokemon go you get up move your body  there so how do we move our bodies a  little bit without needing to move our  bodies too far and how do we translate  that into a meaningful expression in the  virtual space incidentally Seattle is  rich with machop with nary an oddish to  be seen is it strange that one of the  things I'm most excited to bring home is  a fighting-type pokémon that can  challenge the Dragonite we've lured 420  leaves of the gym down the street one of  the best dimensions of packs of any con  really is the cosplay not just for the  spectacular craftsmanship and creativity  on display but because it gives you a  sense of what's connecting with people  what are the characters the outlines the  stories that they see themselves in or  that they want to inhabit to literally  embody as VR lets us swap our skin with  virtual bodies cosplayers rework virtual  bodies onto their skin  outside golden British Columbia is a  segment of highway a twisted narrow  stretch of road that clings to the edge  of the mountains as it winds up and down  the size of the Kicking Horse Canyon  this is the highway you've been driving  with me but the one you're seeing isn't  the one I remember the highway was  terrible with brutally sharp turns  narrow shoulders and insufficient guards  access to the town was routinely cut off  as chunks of road would slide off into  the river below workers spent ten years  chiseling improvements out of the  mountain widening the berth shoring up  the walls and abandoning stretches in  favour of a brand new bridge and road  blasted out of the rock as you drive it  if you know what you're looking for you  can see fragments of the old road bits  and chunks of abandoned blacktop but  it's almost impossible now to really  remember what the old road looked like  what it felt like to take its sharp  corners stuck in a narrow lane will a  tanker truck past going the other way  I remember that highway but I don't I  remember it viscerally I remember how it  felt even as the path the dips and  curves and exact placement fades years  back I spent time in Australia where I  took a road trip from Brisbane down to  Sydney I spent several days with my  Australian friends coaching me through  the process of relearning how to drive  how to be confident reversing one's  instincts of which shoulder to check  over and remembering which hand  triggered the indicator in which hand  turned on the wipers logically I know I  drove on the left hand side for the  entire trip for Brisbane through port  McCurry and down to Sydney because that  is actually the way that Australians  drive but it's not the way I remember it  after I returned home to Canada and  reacquainted myself with right hand  driving going through many of the same  mistakes with the wipers and the signals  my visual memories began to morph the  snippets of highway that haven't been  erased have all been reversed when I  visualized cruising through Gold Coast  on a drizzly November afternoon it's all  flipped I'm sitting on the left-hand  side of the car going down the  right-hand side of the road I still turn  left out of my apartment to get to  Hungry Jack's but now in my mind I turn  left from the right-hand lane I'm not  entirely sure how I want to encapsulate  this year's packs experience because I'm  still not quite sure what this year's  packs experience will turn out to be  there is what it was standing in lines  playing demos people watching good  conversations pin trading walking  getting rained on getting depressed  getting excited  enjoying Seattle and pining for Calgary  but how much of that will translate into  what it will be a week or a month from  now the impressions that are handed out  are inherently suspect delicately  curated for maximum impact and devoid of  the messy charms of a more engaged  experience many border on illusory  apparitions that resemble the idea of a  future experience while containing  little if any of the ingredients that  experience will actually be made of they  are for good and ill roads that will be  paved over  you
 

